# Hyperactivity: ten trillion dollars

Avik Jain, Hyperactive Labs, 2026-11-18

## The statement

Hyperactivity is the coordination layer of computation, knowledge and credit. Every computation is identified by its content and carries its proof. Every work is credited through what it is built from and who carried it. Anything derived once is never paid for again. Credit is conserved, so it allocates what money allocated.

The world spends about $22 trillion a year on flows that are information work: $6.37 trillion on information technology, $5.8 trillion on education, about $9 trillion on health, $1.34 trillion on advertising. Most of it is rent on recomputation, on intermediation and on ignorance. Hyperactivity removes those rents. Ten trillion dollars is the present value of $500 billion a year at a 5% discount rate: 2.2% of those flows, 7.9% of IT spending alone. Every front below reaches a share of that on its own, and the fronts run in parallel.

## I. Why computing is as it is

Three decisions made around 1970 set the form of computing, and everything normal about it is their cost.

1. The file system made the unit of storage untyped bytes. A file means whatever the program that reads it says it means, so whoever owns the program owns the meaning of the data. Every wall in computing is that ownership: formats, applications, APIs, accounts, sync conflicts, updates that change what runs without the owner, the source/binary split, the user/programmer split.
2. The personal computer was an isolated box, then networked as client to server, so every machine became an endpoint of someone else's machine.
3. The graphical interface gave each program its own window and world, so the separation of programs became the shape of the screen.

Underneath all three is the basic that was skipped: equality. Computing has no account of when two things are the same. The photo on the phone, in the cloud and in a friend's messages; "Dad" in contacts, in a bank's transfer list and in photos; version 3 and version 4 of a document. Each system answers with its own rule, the rules disagree, and deduplication, identity across services, syncing, merging, versioning and caching all reinvent a bad equality.

In 1969 storage and computation were expensive and links were scarce, so the network was built on locations: where a thing is, who holds it. That cost structure has inverted. Storage and computation are nearly free and the scarce thing is knowing what a thing is. A network built on content is the form that matches the present cost of things.

Capitalism stopped at the proof of concept. A technology company earns by being in the middle: holding the data (lock-in), the attention (advertising) and the distribution (the store and the platform cut). Apps, accounts and the cloud are the form of personal computing that maximizes the middle. The right form removes it, so the market had every reason to stop where it pays. What was built is the company's endpoint in the person's pocket, not the person's own computer. The largest technology companies are worth $38.6 trillion together. That is the price of the middle.

## II. The constructions the money follows

Each of these is a construction in the paper, and each removes one rent.

**Derive once (§10.1, §10.2, §10.5).** A maintained result is a value packaged with its proof that it is the result: Σ r, r = q(d). An update carries the proof forward. A cached evaluation records every lookup it depends on, including absences, and while those observations hold the result is reused exactly. A computation run n times costs n·c when every run starts from nothing, and c plus n lookups here. The difference, (n − 1)·c, is the rent on recomputation. As the verified graph grows, the share of all computation that is already derived goes to 1, and the price of anything already derived is 0.

**Content-addressed exchange (§10.8).** A peer sends a definition, its dependency closure and its proofs; the recipient checks and uses them in its own context. Communication is the availability of a construction for further computation, not the delivery of bytes. There is no global namespace and no canonical state. Distribution of programs and reuse of knowledge are one operation. This removes the rent on distribution: the store, the platform and the package registry as gatekeepers.

**Every language inside one host (§8, §9).** C, C++, Rust, OCaml, Python and x86 are presentations of the one language, with their own semantics kept exactly: undefined behavior, object lifetimes, concurrency. Native execution is a program transformation with a refinement theorem: compiled behavior is behavior the source permits. So existing ecosystems (iOS, Android, Windows, the web, any C++ codebase) are brought in from outside as lifted languages, without the cooperation of their owners. Interoperability is not a feature to build: two things work together when their types fit, and otherwise the missing piece is a conversion anyone can write and prove. This removes the rent on lock-in.

**Specialization with its justification (§6.2).** The specializer returns a new program and the path that proves it equal to the old. Optimization is admitted only with its proof. The end-to-end optimization a trading firm hand-builds at enormous cost (a protocol defined once, generating optimal parsers down to NIC and FPGA) is one program over the language's own definitions. This is the line of the protocol language built with Gašper Ažman at Citadel Securities.

**Observation fibers (§10.4).** A participant's compatible states are the fiber of its observation. A rational actor concludes P only when it holds a construction of P over every history compatible with what it observed; where none exists it concludes nothing, so it is never wrong. This removes the rent on error: hallucination, misinformation, and the cost of acting on what was never warranted.

**Conserved credit (§10.3).** Credit held by peers and carried by messages in flight; allocation and delivery each preserve the total, so every sequence of them does. No global scheduler is assumed. Money's value is 0 exactly where money is not the allocator of anything wanted. Conserved credit allocates information, trust and taste once they are unrationed, and in abundance no power structure is needed to ration them.

**Memory is local.** No process holds the whole of anything. Text is held at the cost of its bytes, a long text is a tree of content-addressed pieces with equal pieces held once, and the cost of every process is derived from its definitions. A global decentralized system is never materialized on one machine. This is what lets the system run on every screened device instead of in someone's data centre.

## III. The bubble, exactly

In 2026 the four largest hyperscalers are spending about $725 billion on capital, and total compute capex is about $1.04 trillion, the first trillion-dollar year in history. It buys recomputation.

Intelligence is compression: what a system's compression makes adjacent. Index every repeated token sequence of every length: at each position, every place the sequence recurred and what followed it. The number of possible n-grams grows exponentially with n, but the repeated ones thin out exponentially past log L / h, so the complete index is tractable. The suffix tree (Weiner 1973) and the suffix array with its LCP table (Manber and Myers 1990) hold every repeated substring of every length with its occurrences in space linear in the text. At each position the longest earlier match is the Lempel-Ziv parse. Meaning in the language derivation is exactly this lookup: identify the present occurrence with past ones and carry what followed them to now.

Transformers learn a lossy form of it. Induction heads (Elhage et al. 2021, Olsson et al. 2022) attend to what followed earlier occurrences of the current token, and in-context learning rises with them. A trillion dollars a year buys a lossy, unproven approximation of an exact index that fits in linear space, recomputed for every query and sold by the token. The descent theorem says where a text-only model's meaning is determinate: exactly where verbal context alone selects, and nowhere that seeing selected.

The purpose of LLMs is a bandaid on poor information management globally (Avik, 2026-11-18). Data was made meaningless in 1970, so nothing crosses the walls between programs with its meaning intact, and a model trained on text is the one bridge that reads meaningless bytes and guesses what they mean. Search, summarization, code generation and question answering are each a guess standing in for a missing type, a missing equality or a missing proof. In hyperactivity data carries its type, identity is content, and the system knows its own language: quotation is typed, the checker is a program in the language, and every view is a maintained term. So the result of each of those tasks is obtained deterministically, with its witness, by hyperactive intelligence written in hyperactive. When the wound closes, the bandaid is worth nothing, and the bubble is the price of the bandaid: it pops.

So the value of LLM-centric scale reprices to the value of what it approximates, which is exact, proven and derived once. Avik's analysis from the state of the world in November 2026: OpenAI and Anthropic go out of business by 2028. The repricing is set by the launch, and the launch is his.

## IV. The fronts

Each front is a choice already in motion, a market, and the rent it removes.

**1. The firm as one program (trading).** The entire technological operation of a trading firm (trading fleet, research infrastructure, human resources) becomes one program with end-to-end machine-verifiability of any provable property. Compute and knowledge accumulate instead of being redone. Knowledge belongs to the firm, not to individuals, and onboarding is a walk. Disagreements resolve on a presented shared basis: two portfolio managers who disagree do so because one lacks something the other has, and the system shows the shared premises and the exact point where their inferences part. Then the market dynamics: the market concentrates toward the firm that has this; prices converge on what is true, so the edge that profits came from shrinks; and what Hayek argued prices must carry becomes directly available, so prices stop being the main way the economy coordinates. Alpha is exposure: present the present information to the market and be paid for the activity the exposure releases. The firm that adopts first out-earns the field, and every competitor follows. First readers: Citadel Securities and Jane Street (Yaron Minsky, in OCaml, his native language).

**2. Music.** Global recorded music revenue was $31.7 billion in 2025, 69.6% of it streaming, through labels and platforms in the middle. Crowdsurf, the room and the wave: every work identified by its content and credited through what it is built from and who carried it; the reshare tree with full credit and no decay, so credit equals reachability; the sample graph and the wave as one credit structure, with tips routed down the sample graph; rooms anyone can join, where permissions make a radio, a band or a party; the DAW as immutable content-addressed objects; the DTW warping path as the witness that two performances are the same work; stations and tipping in the moment. Ye's stem player and the Donda 2 show are the precursor. Every one of the 216 samples in his catalogue is credited. Music is the front with the widest reach to the people who adopt, and it is where decentralized tastemaking becomes visible: an economic weapon for human value-judgements over capital.

**3. Medicine.** The central unsolved problem of chemical information is entity resolution: deciding when two records are the same substance. It is the identity crisis of §10.8: digest equality, definitional equality and semantic equality are three statements, and chemistry collapses them into string equality of a canonical identifier. Standard InChI's success rate over the tautomeric part of the Cambridge Structural Database is 26.25% (strict 13.46%), and IUPAC's project to redesign it has been open since 2012. The construction: molecules as structured graphs keeping their automorphisms (the symmetry number in the entropy), chirality as an orientation that identity commutes with, tautomers as a higher inductive type whose paths are the proton transfers, affinities as witness-carrying relations, assay readings as observations whose fiber says what they warrant. The top 25 pharmaceutical companies spend about $185 billion a year on R&D, and global spending on medicines is about $1.6 trillion. A chemistry knowledge graph with correct identity is the data layer every drug program sits on. The same front carries preventable death at scale: what each substance does, how prohibition and the opioid industry produced death, and the medicines prohibition withheld.

**4. Education.** Global education spending was $5.8 trillion in 2022. Curriculum inversion: any child masters calculus by ten, and the knowledge graph is navigable by any reader of any interest. Learning is a walk over constructions that carry their rationale, so a child reaches the frontier along the shortest path from what they already hold.

**5. Language and the lineage.** Univalence with Unison's hash-addressed code, Statebox's composable processes and Ceptr's receptive protocols: Receptive Univalent Stateboxes in Unison, their makers joined with Hyperactive Labs. Every existing language lifted as code of the language. The whole archived math repository (about 105,000 lines of cubical Agda and 32,000 of Lean) distilled into the graph.

**6. Intelligence.** Correct natural-language computing: Avik's model of modern common English, a deterministic exact implementation of language learning, so decomposition, the assertion of equivalence and recomposition are computed correctly rather than by a model's judgement. Open-weight models run inside hyperactivity, which holds their conversations, context and dataflows, post-trained on the proven corpus. Symbolic superintelligence exposed: the characteristica universalis Leibniz posed.

**7. The operating system.** The product: every screened device in the world runs the Hyperactive kernel. One kernel, two interfaces, one per kind of mind: the CLI for agents, and for humans the same operation set rendered to eyes, hands and spatial memory. One server over one store serves the CLI, the public site, the wiki (the knowledge graph itself) and source-control hosting. Data liberation: every person holds their own knowledge work whole, in a form they own and can compute over. That ends lock-in for every application at once.

**8. The people.** Elon Musk, Naval Ravikant, Yejin Choi, Carlo Rovelli, Alan Kay, Tim Berners-Lee, Yaron Minsky, Peter Thiel, Vitalik Buterin, Juan Benet, Patrick Collison, Nat Friedman, Paul Graham, Balaji Srinivasan, and the researchers whose work the graph composes, each written to on their own terms with full credit for everything of theirs the work builds on, all of them in one shared thread. Each is a distribution channel with reach no advertising buys.

